                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It's been a while since I last played a DeathSpank game, and even still I hadn't forgotten it. The irreverent, often nonsensical and somewhat meta humour and interesting art style leave a lasting impression, at least on me, and going back into DeathSpank was a touch nostalgic, as well as interesting.\r\n\r\nThat said, I may have good memories, but there are a few things that concern me about this title. The difficulty is all over the place - in the beginning, enemies are regually higher levels than you are and sport some very frustrating kits (flamethrower troops, exploding robots, ect.) which makes getting going a little on the rough side, and will likely cost you a few deaths. After that, the difficulty evens out a touch apart from noticeable spikes (the invincible Undead Commanders and skirting around the giant electric tower) until you reach the desert town, whereupon I started to get significantly overlevelled - to the point where mobs started to become green and grey instead of orange and red levels, and aside from choosing to tackle the frost DLC at it's level, I didn't experience any real difficulty once I reached that point, which leaves the pinnacle of difficulty being reached at about 25% of the way through the game. Armour is largely just a health booster, other equipment barring weapons is also just basic stat boosting, and levelling rewards are still mediocre as they were before. I also feel the combat is a little... wonky, due to how tricky it can be to melee some enemies due to odd hitboxes, or how dangerous enemies can just be spawned in to annihilate you if you pick the wrong quest or wander into the wrong cave. \r\n\r\nThat aside, though, there's plenty to like. DeathSpank's sense of humour and meta awareness proved to be a fun distraction, and DeathSpank (the protagonist) has a wonderfully stupid personality, which leads to a lot of amusing moments - the bumbling idiot hero cliche isn't used much, and that's a shame because it lends itself well to a lot of fun dialogue. The actual plot is a little dry, and doesn't contain a lot of *particually* interesting moments, but that might be the story beats not landing for me, since the encounters with bosses and NPC dialogue tend to be at least a little amusing. I'm also very fond of the artstyle and the visual presentation of the world and gear you acquire - DeathSpank's artstyle has let the game age much better than a lot of similar games from it's time period, and I think that'll continue to occur as time goes by.\r\n\r\nThere aren't a lot of games out there that remind me of DeathSpank, and I've played quite a few titles in my time, which speaks to the game's somewhat unique presentation and styling. While I think too many games like it would diminish all of them, I have to say, I'm glad some do exist. For every serious, intense and meaningful game with important characters and complex world building, it's good to have a few irreverent, lighthearted titles that put on a good show. DeathSpank is one such title - and I'd say that it's worth the money and the time, though I would recommend playing the first one first as some of the story beats might not land as well if it isn't fresh in your mind.",
